-- title:
--   The finite stages of the adaptive tree construction
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. The tree is built level by level from candidate records: each candidate is a generated map attached to a parent node together with a norm-one functional chosen after the map, and every candidate, including those that later receive coefficient zero, consumes a global ordinal $n$ with budget $2^{-(n+9)}$. Fix normalized minimal data, its resolvent anchor $a$, a terminal sequence and an ultrafilter.
--
--   1. **Run tolerance.** At a stage of depth $k$ with base ordinal $n$, $\tau=2^{-k}\beta(n)$.
--   2. **Advance.** The selected level extension with this tolerance becomes the new frontier. The depth increases by $1$, the base ordinal increases by the number of all candidates chosen (retained or not), and every child records its proper ancestor maps.
--   3. **Stages.** Starting from the root frontier with the identity map, stages are defined by natural recursion and keep the same anchor.
--   4. **Tower.** The frontiers and extensions of all stages form a frontier tower. All its maps are generated, and every child average at the anchor returns within the stage tolerance:
--   $$
--   \Bigl\|\sum_cw_cg_c(a)-a\Bigr\|<\tau .
--   $$
